WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.680
This won’t be a spoiler review since I feel like&nbsp;
there isn’t a major rush for people to see this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:09.180
show and the good word of mouth surrounding&nbsp;
Andor is slowly bringing more attention to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:14.100
it. Within Andor’s praise, you have probably&nbsp;
heard that this is the best star wars content&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:19.920
in a long time and technically that is true, but&nbsp;
to be fair there isn’t a lot of competition. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:25.200
Book of Boba Fett was pretty bad, Obi-Wan was a&nbsp;
nightmare behind the scenes and on screen, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:31.080
the Mandalorian is good at times, but it has its&nbsp;
own issues. Andor is a clear standout in quality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:36.480
however, don’t go in expecting a magnum opus&nbsp;
that is going to blow your socks off. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:41.280
is a slow-paced show that takes its time with&nbsp;
characters and plot progression and everything it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:47.040
sets out to achieve is done superbly and pretty&nbsp;
much flawlessly. This show is a spy thriller,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:51.960
with a big emphasis on drama. And with that kind&nbsp;
of emphasis, you need to create a colorful cast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:58.380
of characters. Ironically out of all the Star Wars&nbsp;
content, Andor has the most interesting ensemble.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:03.540
Which is really sad considering Obi-Wan just&nbsp;
came out and that show was so dull in terms of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:09.000
character work. For Andor, we have Cassian who&nbsp;
is a good POV character for the audience since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:14.580
he’s getting ushered into this new rebel world. He&nbsp;
feels very compelling because he’s so reluctant to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:20.640
join the cause and is instead swayed by his own&nbsp;
motivations. He’s not blindly joining the rebels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:25.860
for unknown reasons the writers don’t elaborate&nbsp;
on, but he acts realistically to serve himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:31.140
This in turn makes the plot a bit unpredictable&nbsp;
because it doesn’t go down that stereotypical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:36.480
path of the protagonist turning into an immediate&nbsp;
good guy. Then the supporting cast I think is rock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:41.700
solid and there are even some Game of Thrones&nbsp;
alumni who show up like Lord Royce, the Waif,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:47.700
and Qyburn who all do an excellent job. As well as&nbsp;
some other notable supporting characters like Ebon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:53.220
Moss, and i’m not sure how to say his last name,&nbsp;
who was amazing in The Bear, and Andy Serkis. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:58.980
adore Andy Serkis and I really hope he gets more&nbsp;
live-action work because this man can act and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:03.480
had an engrossing monologue that’s filled with&nbsp;
so much emotion. Then this isn’t even getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:08.700
into the best part of the cast which is Stellan&nbsp;
Skarsgard. If this guy chooses a project then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:13.560
I’m immediately interested in it given that he’s&nbsp;
been on a hot streak and if he’s choosing Andor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:19.140
of all shows to be in, then there has to be&nbsp;
a reason. He is a world-class actor and any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:24.420
line of dialogue he delivers is done with so much&nbsp;
weight and gravitas. His character in this show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:29.160
is by far the most engaging because he’s at the&nbsp;
center of the rebellion so any plot development&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:34.860
around him is always riveting. This also ties into&nbsp;
mon Mothma because she’s also at the center of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:40.620
rebellion big bang. Every scene she is in has so&nbsp;
much tension because she has to act very carefully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:46.500
so she doesn’t set off any red flags. This ties&nbsp;
in a more political and scheming aspect to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:51.240
show that it kind of shares with Game of Thrones.&nbsp;
It actually makes the conflict of starting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:56.100
rebel network a surprising delight because of&nbsp;
how difficult it is here. Characters don’t just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:01.380
snap their fingers and spawn in allies like in the&nbsp;
movies, because that actually takes time and lots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:06.600
of hard work. This is where the meat of Andor&nbsp;
is and since the writing is really well done,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:11.880
it excels in these dramatic aspects. Probably&nbsp;
because this show is being passionately made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:18.120
by Tony Gilroy who also wrote Rogue one and the&nbsp;
bourne trilogy, so the man is GOATED. This isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:22.740
a case of Obi-Wan where Disney forces it into&nbsp;
existence and butchers the original direction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:28.080
of the story to fill the mini-series format. So&nbsp;
the fact that this show both has a great and solid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:33.360
direction from a creative leader who is dedicated&nbsp;
to the project, and great actors who want to be in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:38.820
this show speaks volumes for the quality of Andor.&nbsp;
Along with the fact that this actually feels like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:43.740
a feature-length show. Because Disney Plus shows&nbsp;
have been gaining the reputation of being like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:49.680
30 minutes long once you disregard the intro and&nbsp;
outro. Andor has a few episodes of that duration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:54.780
but most of them are about 40 to 50 minutes long.&nbsp;
So that’s another sign that the creators are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.780 --> 00:04:00.300
putting care into the story instead of rushing to&nbsp;
a conclusion. There is no filler like the endless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:05.220
amount of side quests in The Mandalorian and half&nbsp;
of the book of boba fett being flashbacks. Andor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:10.020
follows different perspectives in this expansive&nbsp;
universe and it all intertwines in a glorious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:15.540
fashion. Now going into this show it starts out&nbsp;
pretty slow and I honestly had a hard time getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:20.340
through the first two episodes given that they had&nbsp;
to set-up a lot of material like dozen characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:25.980
background and motivations, and Cassian’s&nbsp;
backstory. The plot wasn’t that engaging at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:30.060
that point, but it evolves and becomes more&nbsp;
interesting once Cassian becomes entangled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:34.320
with the rebels. The second he gets into&nbsp;
a room with Stellan Skarsgard’s character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:40.200
it becomes really fun. So if you do decide to&nbsp;
try the show, at least get to episode 3 because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:45.780
the first 3 episodes have one big arc. And the&nbsp;
other mini plot arcs that Cassian goes down get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:51.300
even better. Also, Andor doesn’t appeal to people&nbsp;
who want constant action and lightsaber battles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:56.520
It has action in small quantity and the show earns&nbsp;
it by properly building it up and having extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.520 --> 00:05:02.220
satisfying payoff. As an example, Cassian meets&nbsp;
a crew of people and they decide to do a heist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:06.120
And instead of having a fast pace where right&nbsp;
after meeting them they cut to the mission,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:11.820
Cassian instead has to learn the operation and&nbsp;
in turn meets the characters. Everyone is very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:16.500
fleshed out because of this and they all have&nbsp;
compelling backstories. So when we inevitably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:21.180
get to the action, which is also really well done&nbsp;
from a writing standpoint and a visual standpoint,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:26.460
we actually care about these people. Another&nbsp;
big aspect of this show in terms of plot and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:31.260
writing is that there isn’t a constant barrage&nbsp;
of terrible mistakes. There’s no Leia getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:35.340
chased through the woods by bounty hunters who&nbsp;
can’t catch her, or characters surviving getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:40.560
skewered by a lightsaber. Andor feels realistic&nbsp;
and grounded and there is nothing in the writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:45.660
department that compromises that feeling. Because&nbsp;
of this and the more slow pace, the show feels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:50.340
significantly more gritty and foreboding.&nbsp;
And since we are slowing down, the writers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:55.440
also give the villains over in the empire a lot&nbsp;
of screen time. At first, I was kind of scared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:59.640
these scenes would be dull since it’s a bunch of&nbsp;
old people talking about security and politics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:05.160
but that isn’t the case. The rendition of the&nbsp;
empire here is very competent and makes them seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:10.020
much more menacing because they are constantly on&nbsp;
the heels of our heroes and tracking them. That’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:14.040
why these scenes are so engaging because they&nbsp;
are directly tied to the plot, they are setting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:19.020
up new and interesting characters, there’s even&nbsp;
conflict amongst the ranks to add even more spice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:24.300
and it’s actively intriguing because we’ve never&nbsp;
seen this kind of meticulous detail before in Star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:29.280
Wars. That’s one major aspect about this show&nbsp;
that I love so much because Star Wars has such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:33.960
a rich and immersive universe, but because&nbsp;
of bad past writers on different projects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:39.540
they essentially squander the potential of the&nbsp;
IP and it becomes very bland as a result. Tony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:44.400
Gilroy brings in that much-needed feeling of depth&nbsp;
and fully explores everything tied to the rebels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:49.920
the empire, and how each planet is being impacted.&nbsp;
Thankfully, this detail within the world builds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:55.020
the empire as stronger antagonists and doesn’t&nbsp;
portray them as buffoons. It makes them feel like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.020 --> 00:07:00.240
a real and tangible threat that is pursuing our&nbsp;
heroes. Which thankfully there isn’t an annoying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:04.950
amount of stormtroopers constantly missing to&nbsp;
make the villain un-antagonizing like in Obi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.950 --> 00:07:10.560
Wan. Because when shit goes down, some characters&nbsp;
that you come to like will die. The show in that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:14.760
regard isn’t afraid of killing characters and&nbsp;
that’s refreshing because it doesn’t give off the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:19.800
notion of plot armor that destroys any semblance&nbsp;
of tension. It makes the action significantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:24.660
more intense despite us knowing that Cassian will&nbsp;
survive until the events of Rogue One. Because we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:28.260
don’t know the outcome of different side&nbsp;
characters and how certain story elements will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.260 --> 00:07:33.420
play out. That’s why the show has to establish&nbsp;
likable characters and they do that expertly.

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:38.580
Going into a more technical and production point&nbsp;
of view for Andor, the show still doesn’t show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:44.700
any flaws. For starters, every set piece is really&nbsp;
refreshing and is much needed since almost every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:49.620
show so far has been set in the dullness of&nbsp;
Tatooine. Like we have a mining planet that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:53.640
is being destabilized by the empire, a more&nbsp;
mountainous planet that looks like the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:58.800
parts of Scotland, Coruscant is back, and all&nbsp;
the interiors for the empire are so eerie and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:03.540
corporate and is so devoid of life given that&nbsp;
everything is washed out in white. Essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:08.040
contrasting the rest of the production design in&nbsp;
the show. This was really the first time that I’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:12.480
been impressed by the sets for Disney+ Star Wars&nbsp;
content, and I’m happy that they are giving the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:16.920
show a bigger sense of scale than The Mandalorian&nbsp;
as an example. If they were using the volume&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:21.960
regularly then they easily fooled me because it&nbsp;
looks like they are using practical locations and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:26.820
using VFX as a form of set extension. I tend to&nbsp;
prefer this more because it makes the world feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:32.400
tangible when you really build it. It’s infinitely&nbsp;
more impressive to me and is awe-inspiring. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:36.060
volume used well can perfectly blend into&nbsp;
an already existing collection of good sets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:40.740
but when you primarily use the volume it&nbsp;
can become a little noticeable. For the VFX,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:45.180
this isn’t a show that requires a lot of it&nbsp;
since there isn’t a big action set piece in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:50.340
every episode, so it’s primarily used to ground&nbsp;
us in the world through environments. Disney is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:54.900
not sparing any expense and the VFX is very&nbsp;
immersive because it brings a staggering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.900 --> 00:09:00.480
amount of quality to the details of each world.&nbsp;
And even when there is an emphasis on VFX like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:05.460
this tie fighter flying overhead, it’s very&nbsp;
realistic. Like just watch this brief clip.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:17.520
Through the interaction with the environment and&nbsp;
the characters being scared to death and being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:22.560
deafened by the loud noises establishes this&nbsp;
as being real. Then when the show decides to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:28.140
go big with VFX it really blows you away with the&nbsp;
striking visual design. Andor is just a beautiful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:33.420
show and even the cinematography doesn’t miss a&nbsp;
single beat. The show hooked me really early on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:38.460
with this long take focused on a close-up of&nbsp;
Cassian’s face as he’s being pestered by two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:43.800
officers. It immediately allowed Diego Luna&nbsp;
to impress us with his great acting and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:50.220
was a very bold shot. So right away, the show&nbsp;
establishes a high bar for cinematography. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:55.920
music is also a major standout as well. This isn’t&nbsp;
like Obi-Wan where you probably have no memory of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.920 --> 00:10:01.320
what the main theme was, because here, it’s very&nbsp;
memorable. The composer for Andor is also the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:06.720
same composer for Succession and the main theme&nbsp;
for that show is iconic. So to say the least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:11.520
he brings just as much talent to Andor. I&nbsp;
particularly love the emphasis being put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:15.780
on violins and it gives a lot of the music&nbsp;
within this soundtrack such a beautiful and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:20.100
uplifting endearing feeling. It portrays&nbsp;
the notion of breaking free of tyranny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:25.020
and gaining freedom. Each episode opens&nbsp;
with a long logo of Andor and honestly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:30.720
I haven’t skipped one because I immediately am&nbsp;
put in a wonder-like trance by the music. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:34.920
and this soundtrack goes down some interesting&nbsp;
routes and the composer, Nicholas Britell, even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:40.200
tries out a more synth style at times. It’s a very&nbsp;
refreshing soundtrack and is pretty close to The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.200 --> 00:10:44.640
Mandalorian in terms of quality. The Mandalorian&nbsp;
to me in terms of music is still my favorite,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:50.640
but that’s because Ludwig Goransson is one of my&nbsp;
favorite composers. So yeah, everything within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:55.500
this show from the acting, character work,&nbsp;
writing, different and fun story arcs, tone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.500 --> 00:11:00.720
tense atmosphere, and everything on the production&nbsp;
side of the coin is done with so much expertise to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:06.240
the point that it deserves to be watched. This&nbsp;
is a show made with so much passion and care put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:11.280
into the story. It may not have characters people&nbsp;
love going in to immediately sell it, but it does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:16.800
a magnificent job of creating a rich roster of&nbsp;
characters that you will easily come to love. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:21.120
was very hesitant going into the show but I ended&nbsp;
up binge-watching almost the whole show in one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:25.080
sitting. Thank you for watching and let me&nbsp;
know if you guys have been enjoying Andor.

